{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-portability","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"New implement-bootstrap.sh code must stay Bash 3.2 portable per BASH_AUTHORING.md, yet the diff introduces [[ =~ ]] validation and complex mktemp+subshell chains whose portability and cleanup behavior warrant dedicated review.","prompt_body":"Audit new and changed shell code in scripts/implement-bootstrap.sh, scripts/write-session-env.sh, and skills/implement/scripts/post-tracking-issue.sh for Bash 3.2 incompatibilities per BASH_AUTHORING.md. Specifically check whether the [[ ! \"$UPSTREAM_REPO_OPT\" =~ ... ]] validation added to implement-bootstrap.sh main() is permissible under the Bash 3.2 target (=~ is available from Bash 3.1, but confirm no 4+ construct is lurking nearby), and flag any other 4+ constructs introduced in the diff. Verify that the mktemp temp files allocated in run_larch_log_init (init_err) and rename_to_implementing (rename_log) are cleaned up on every exit path including early returns and tracking_init_failed() calls. Check that the $() subshell in run_larch_log_init captures stdout and stderr separately without mixing streams, and that $? is captured immediately after the subshell with no intervening commands that could clobber the exit code. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"kv-protocol-consistency","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Five new KV keys emitted by emit_final_tail must be parsed by SKILL.md's _ib_kv_scan space-tokenizing loop and then exported; any gap between emit and parse sides creates a silent protocol break that stalls /implement runs without a visible error.","prompt_body":"Cross-check every key emitted by emit_final_tail and emit_infra_kv_block in scripts/implement-bootstrap.sh against the _ib_kv_scan case statement in the Step 0 Bash block of skills/implement/SKILL.md. Specifically verify that BRANCH_SELECTED, DEFERRED, STALL_TRACKING, and IMPLEMENT_BAIL_REASON each appear in both the emit and the parse sides, and that the export statement at the end of the Bash block includes all newly parsed variables. Confirm that the bootstrap tracking bail routing table in SKILL.md covers every IMPLEMENT_BAIL_REASON value the script can emit (adopted-issue-closed, adopted-issue-is-pr, tracking-init-failed) and maps each to a concrete routing action. Check whether an unknown STATE value from get-issue-state.sh (not OPEN, CLOSED, or IS_PR=true) falls into the exit-2 guard in phase_tracking and whether SKILL.md's bail table accounts for that exit-2 outcome. Also check whether DEFERRED or STALL_TRACKING being emitted as empty strings rather than false (e.g., when the tracking phase is never entered) could silently misroute the orchestrator's routing logic. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"sentinel-lifecycle-ordering","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"Branch 2 runs larch-log.sh init before post-tracking-issue.sh before sentinel write, creating partial-success states on retry; Invariant #4 requires idempotency across these steps but the deferred (POSTED=false) path leaves the manifest initialized with no sentinel, and the retry semantics for that state are not explicitly tested.","prompt_body":"Trace the parent-issue.md sentinel lifecycle through each code path in phase_tracking in scripts/implement-bootstrap.sh. In Branch 2, larch-log.sh init runs before post-tracking-issue.sh; if post returns POSTED!=true (DEFERRED=true path), the manifest was already initialized but no sentinel was written — confirm that on a subsequent retry Branch 2 will re-call larch-log.sh init, that the real script's idempotency guarantee (UNCHANGED=true on an existing manifest) prevents double-init corruption, and that BRANCH_SELECTED=branch-2-adopt is still correctly emitted in this deferred case. In Branch 1, when larch-log.sh init fails, STALL_TRACKING is set but the sentinel file is NOT cleared — verify this is intentional (preserving ISSUE_NUMBER for Step 18's stalled rename) and check whether a subsequent retry with the stale sentinel present will re-enter Branch 1 and attempt init again rather than falling through to Branch 2. Also verify that the rm -f \"$sentinel\" call in the POSTED!=true block is truly a no-op (post-tracking-issue.sh only writes the sentinel on success, so at that code point no sentinel should exist), and flag if there is any scenario where a sentinel could already be present at that rm -f call. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
